A||||8||dogtired||Starter Spacer||||10||
Z||000000||dogtired||12-17-1999||02:52 PM||||Replaced starter on my V6 cause it wasn't hitting the flywheel everytime. Starter would just spin. Anyway the one I put on it had a smaller spacer, not as thick. It sounded awful, but would start the car every time. I replaced the thinner spacer with the one I took off the car. Still starts every time and still sounds bad, (but not quite so loud) like it is really hitting the flywheel HARD?<BR>OK the question, is there any procedure to tell how thick a spacer I should be using? Should I try 2 spacers? It starts everytime but I don't like the way it sounds? What do you think?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000001||DRH||12-17-1999||04:15 PM||||GM uses shims (spacers) to compensate for tolerances in manufacturing. Any time you change starters, you may need more or less shims.<P>The following is paraphrasing from memory from the 88 Fiero service manual: There should be 0.5mm clearance between the top of the starter gear tooth and the bottom of the flywheel (whatever you call the space between the teeth) when the starter is engaged. Make sure the tooth is centered when measuring. Add shims (spacers) as required to get that clearance. A washer or other suitable fashioned spacer may be used if shims aren't available. To increase the clearance, add spacers to both sides (typical shim with a slot in one end and a hole in the other). In the unlikely event you need to decrease the clearance, add spacers to the outside bolt only.<P>I've checked this before with the engine out using a piece of mechanical pencil lead (I think a small paper clip is approx. the right diameter also). Probably a lot harder to do with the engine in! You can remove the solenoid from the top of the starter and engage it by pulling out on the piece that fits inside the solenoid.<P>Don't know how to post a file yet... If you can't follow what I'm talking about measuring let me know and I'll email you a sketch.<P>Good luck, David.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||12-17-1999||04:21 PM||||DRH-If you're trying to figure out how to download an image to your post, just click on the "Pennocks Image Poster" icon on the bottom of the screen.  You just have to download the program and follow the rest of the directions.  It's really a piece of cake.  Just remember that when you post an image, make sure that the title of the image doesn't have any spaces in it.  Instead of spaces, use the "underline" symbol (_).  If you have spaces your image won't post.  I know this from experience.  Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000003||DRH||12-17-1999||06:56 PM||||Dogtired, tried emailing to the address you sent from and got unknown user back from your postmaster... tried account listed here and got data format error... followed monkeyman's advise and downloaded software... couldn't get it to work either... (I'm behind a firewall, has caused problems with other i-net software, I'll check later when I have more time) With this run of luck I'm hoping the Fiero starts when I leave  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Since I can't seem to get you a pic, hopefully it'll become obvious when you look at it. What you are trying to do is set how deeply the starter gear meshes with the flywheel. Just make sure you are measuring at the closest point.<P>You may already know this but to get the solenoid off: <BR>- disconnect at least one battery cable <BR>- remove starter ??? (may not have to do this) <BR>- remove bolt/screw that makes electrical connection between solenoid and starter <BR>- remove two bolt/screws that hold solenoid to starter <BR>- rotate solenoid slightly to disengage tab hooked in starter <BR>- pull it off (there is a spring in it but not strong enough to fly apart or anything) <BR>- now you can slide the starter gear in and out by pulling/pushing on the <BR>piece that was inside the solenoid. <P>The alternate "seat of the pants" method is to keep gradually adding spacers until it sounds right. If it starts sounding worse you may already have too much clearance. Try no spacers, then gradually adding spacers on the outside only (tilts the starter into the flywheel) until it sounds right.<P>Let me know how it turns out. <P>David. <BR>||||reg||1
